{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Questions to Help Decide When to Track Defects"}, {"type": "position", "content": 5}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "B2.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to talk about defect tracking. "}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe ways of solving problems and getting things done in written and spoken situations and in work and social contexts. Train the system users on application structuring and management, according to the established plan and user profile, according to the organization policies."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Integrates a wider range of written and spoken language skills and knowledge when dealing with problems and difficulties in the workplace."}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Exchanges information on managing a proposed application, using available hardaware for an application, and technical reports on an IT solution."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n8le01ob05re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Causative get and have"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We use <span class=\"strong\"> causatives </span> when we want to express that a person is causing something to happen."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Every organization should have their defects tracked in order to get them fixed."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I suggest having some questions addressed to get to know more about how this process should be carried out."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>We can use the verb <span class=\"strong\"> get </span>; or the verb <span class=\"strong\"> have </span> when we want to express the idea of getting something done.<br> To use <span class=\"strong\"> causative verbs</span>, we are going to follow the next structure:<br><br> <div class=\"center_text\"> Causative Verb (<span class=\"strong\"> Get / Have</span>) + Object + <span class=\"strong\"> Verb in Past Participle</span>  </div><br> </p> <p>&nbsp;Example:</p> <ul> <li><span class=\"italic\">The manager is going to <span class=\"strong\"> have</span> the defects properly <span class=\"strong\"> tracked</span>.<br>I <span class=\"strong\"> got</span>the data <span class=\"strong\"> collected </span>last week."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "<strong>Understanding the Pareto Principle</strong>"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "The Pareto Principle states that most things in life are not distributed evenly. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "This principle originally referred to the fact that 80% of Italy's wealth belonged to the 20% of the population. Nevertheless, this principle can be applied to many other situations in life. For instance, in software development, we need to take into account that not all the bugs are going to cause the same damage to the software. Most likely, around 20% of the bugs are going to be the ones responsible for causing crashes, and the remaining 80% will either cause minor imperfections or may even pass unnoticed. <br><br>  For example, only a 10% of the total applications developed in a year could have a considerable amount of downloads or the first 20% of your time reading an article is going to get you through the most important concepts. Knowing this can be very useful; once we identify the portion where the most effort, reward, or output is located, we will know where to focus most of our attention on. <br><br>  Once an organization finds that the 20% percent of the defects that are causing the software to crash are originated in the coding process, then, this organization can make sure to pay more attention to this area and improve its processes. <br>"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "lag behind "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to stay behind, not to be at expected or required level"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(phrasal verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: So, the software development team was lagging behind because they were tracking defects?"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n8le01ob05aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "enrich "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to add more value or detailed information to something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I thought having detailed information entered could enrich the database and facilitate the fixing process."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n8le01ob05aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "pass unnoticed "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to be ignored or not noticed"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Some important defects were passing unnoticed."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n8le01ob05aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "inject defects into a product "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to originate defects in a product at a moment of the development process"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: When are these defects injected into the product?"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n8le01ob05aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "facilitate "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to make something easier "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I thought having detailed information entered could enrich the database and facilitate the fixing process."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n8le01ob05aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "overlook "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to ignore, neglect"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: they started to overlook some defects to save time. Therefore, some important defects were passing unnoticed."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n8le01ob05aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for information about Pareto Principle."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Research about different examples of it in Software development."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Make a list of them."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write a short text explaining how it explains some cases in software development."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I look for relevant information about Pareto Principle."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I explain Pareto Principle in software development."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I share my thoughts with accurate vocabulary. "}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can say why the principle of collecting more defects may not work in some cases. "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can list some questions an organization should ask to decide when to start tracking defects. "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can talk about the relation of the Pareto Principle to defect tracking."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": " Do you think that all defects are equally important and should be tracked under the same parameters? Why?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "How do you collect data for defect tracking processes?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}],